DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnmm utility displays symbolic information in the object files, exe‐
     cutables, and object library files named by its arguments.  Lack of sym‐
     bolic information in an otherwise valid input file, is not considered to
     be an error.  If no files are specified on the command line, nnmm will at‐
     tempt to read _a_._o_u_t.

     The nnmm utility recognizes the following options:

     ----ddeebbuugg--ssyymmss
                  Display all symbols, including debugger-only symbols.

     ----ddeeffiinneedd--oonnllyy
                  Display only defined symbols.

     ----ddeemmaannggllee[=_s_t_y_l_e]
                  Decode (demangle) low-level symbol names into human-readable
                  names.  Supported values for argument _s_t_y_l_e are ‘auto’,
                  ‘gnu-v2’, ‘gnu-v3’ and ‘arm.’ If argument _s_t_y_l_e is not spec‐
                  ified, it is taken to be ‘auto’.

     ----ddyynnaammiicc    Only display dynamic symbols.  This option is only meaning‐
                  ful for shared libraries.

     ----eexxtteerrnn--oonnllyy
                  Only display information about global (external) symbols.

     ----hheellpp       Display a help message and exit.

     ----ffoorrmmaatt=_f_o_r_m_a_t
                  Display output in the format specified by argument _f_o_r_m_a_t.
                  Supported values for the format argument are ‘bsd’, ‘sysv’,
                  and ‘posix’.  The default output format is ‘bsd’.

     ----lliinnee--nnuummbbeerrss
                  Display the filename and line number associated a symbol us‐
                  ing any debugging information present in the input file.
                  For defined symbols, look up the line number associated with
                  the address of the symbol.  For undefined symbols, look up
                  the line number associated with a relocation entry that
                  refers to the symbol.  If line number information can be de‐
                  termined, it is displayed after other symbol information.

     ----nnoo--ddeemmaannggllee
                  Do not demangle symbol names (default).

     ----nnoo--ssoorrtt    Do not sort symbols.

     ----nnuummeerriicc--ssoorrtt
                  Sort symbols numerically by address instead of alphabeti‐
                  cally by name.

     ----pprriinntt--aarrmmaapp
                  For ar(1) archives, include the index of the archive's mem‐
                  bers.

     ----pprriinntt--ffiillee--nnaammee
                  Write the full pathname or library name of an object on each
                  line, before the rest of the information for a symbol.  If
                  this option is not specified, nnmm will only identify an input
                  file once, before its symbols are listed.

     ----pprriinntt--ssiizzee
                  Print the size of each symbol instead of its value.

     ----rraaddiixx=_r_a_d_i_x
                  Print numeric values using the specified radix.  Supported
                  values for argument _r_a_d_i_x are ‘d’ for decimal, ‘o’ for oc‐
                  tal, and ‘x’ for hexadecimal.

     ----rreevveerrssee--ssoorrtt
                  Reverse the order of the sort.

     ----ssiizzee--ssoorrtt  Sort symbols by size instead of alphabetically by name.

     ----uunnddeeffiinneedd--oonnllyy
                  Display only undefined symbols.

     ----vveerrssiioonn    Display the version identifier for nnmm and exit.

